  "rule": {
    "title": "The passive",
    "explanation": "La pasiva («be» + participio) pone el foco en la acción o en lo que la recibe, no en quién la hace. Es muy habitual en avisos, normas y noticias:\n- «The flight has been delayed.»\n- «Passengers are asked to wait.»\n\nEl verbo «be» cambia de tiempo y el participio no:\n- presente: «Breakfast is served from seven.»\n- pasado: «The airport was built in 1957.»\n- present perfect: «The flight has been cancelled.»\n- futuro: «Your bag will be sent to Dublin.»\n- con modal: «Phones must be switched off.», «It can be done online.»\n\nSi queremos decir quién hace la acción, usamos «by»:\n- «The airport was designed by a famous architect.»\n\nNormalmente no se dice quién lo hace cuando no se sabe, no importa o es evidente:\n- «My bike was stolen.» (no sé quién)\n\nOjo: en español usamos mucho la pasiva refleja con «se» («se construyó en 1957», «se habla inglés»); en inglés, la pasiva: «It was built in 1957», «English is spoken».",
    "table": {
      "headers": [
        "Tiempo",
        "Activa",
        "Pasiva"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Presente",
          "They serve breakfast at seven.",
          "Breakfast is served at seven."
        ],
        [
          "Pasado",
          "They built it in 1957.",
          "It was built in 1957."
        ],
        [
          "Present perfect",
          "They have delayed the flight.",
          "The flight has been delayed."
        ],
        [
          "Futuro",
          "We will send your bag.",
          "Your bag will be sent."
        ],
        [
          "Modal",
          "You must switch off phones.",
          "Phones must be switched off."
        ]
      ]
    }
  },
